# Entity lists, readings and row actions

Let the entity domain and the row configuration decide the control, not the icon or the words on screen. A household list often mixes temperatures, lights, events and helper values. Keep the comparison compact, and do not make every row behave like a switch.

The examples on this page are simulated. They do not call Home Assistant.

<h2 id="readings">Entity, Glance, Sensor and Statistic</h2>

Pick the card for the question. Entity shows one reported state. Glance compares several short states. Entities allows mixed rows. Sensor can add a history graph. Statistic gives a summary for a stated period. A current reading and a mean can show the same number and still be different quantities, so name which one each row is. History graph, Statistics graph and Activity work to different time and data rules. Use [History, activity and statistics](history-and-statistics.html) instead of one general “recent” label.

Use the native formatted state and unit. Keep source names, including long or duplicate ones, and add the area or ID when people need it to tell them apart. Show missing, unavailable, assumed, `null` and zero as set out in [Entity state, confidence and freshness](entity-state.html#ha-ctrl-001-states). Colour supports the text and does not replace it. Do not use row height or a right-aligned value to hide an uncertainty label.

