<h2 id="domain-rows">Domain rows and grouped actions</h2>

Say what each row does. The pinned Home Assistant row dispatcher gives climate, cover, date and time, fan, humidifier, lock, media player, number, select, text, timer, update and valve entities their own controls. Light, switch, remote, siren and vacuum entities use a toggle row. Scene and script rows run an operation, and running it does not leave a lasting on or off state. A sensor or event row only reports. A simple-entity row deliberately avoids the domain control. An unrecognised domain falls back to a simple row, so do not guess a command.

Toggle rows can mean different things. Turning on an automation changes whether it runs. Turning on a light requests a device state. Describe the actual action. Keep native special cases, such as a water heater row using the climate presentation in this release, and do not rewrite the entity domain to match its renderer.

A header toggle or group action must name the entities it can act on. Leave out unsupported targets by the native rules, and list unavailable or rejected members separately. A partial result must never claim the whole list changed. See [Action requests and results](action-results.html#ha-ctrl-002-states).

