<h2 id="special-rows">Attributes and special rows</h2>

| Row kind | What it means and what to show |
| --- | --- |
| Attribute | Name the parent entity and the attribute quantity. An absent attribute is not a numeric zero. |
| Button or buttons | Give each action its own name that includes its target. Do not stretch a row-wide click over nested controls. |
| Conditional | Keep the native condition check and a separate way to reach essential information. |
| Divider or section | A quiet separator or a real heading. It has no control behaviour. |
| Weblink | Name the external destination. Opening a link is not sending a device command. |
| Cast | Name the real playback target and what casting supports. Treat it as an action, not navigation. |
| Text and legacy call-service | Keep reading saved configuration that uses them. In new examples, use the current action wording and do not silently rewrite stored IDs. |

Say what secondary information measures. Last changed is not the time of observation, and an area does not identify a device. An attribute’s unit comes from the attribute itself, not automatically from the parent state’s unit. Let link and value text wrap without covering an action.

<%= example('ha-coverage-rows-attributes') %>

<h2 id="more-info">More info and fallback presentation</h2>

Keep the native More info route reachable by keyboard, and add a visible action where the row itself does something else. A history plot, attributes, controls and entity settings each have their own job. A domain dialog may be unavailable while the name and diagnostics remain useful. Keep the native read-only fallback, the disabled and restored messages and the permission checks.

The coverage inventory records 35 More info control source files, including the default control and the legacy configurator. That does not promise a separate dialog for every platform, and it does not mean every entity has history. Never invent a custom control because the source falls back to the default presentation.

<h2 id="acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

Use a mixed list with a sensor reading zero, an absent attribute, a scene, an unavailable light and a long media title. Check each action against its domain. Test a header action with a rejected member, a read-only override, no history and an unknown domain. Read the list with a screen reader: each value must stay linked to its name, unit and action. Confirm that sorting does not move keyboard focus to another entity.

For the native definitions, see the Home Assistant documentation for the [Entities card](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/entities/) and [Statistic card](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/statistic/), and the pinned frontend source for the [row dispatcher](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/src/panels/lovelace/create-element/create-row-element.ts) and [More info composition](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/src/dialogs/more-info/ha-more-info-info.ts).

<h2 id="evidence">Implementation and evidence boundary</h2>

This is a design reference, not a replacement for native Home Assistant components. The examples use invented records and local feedback. Mapping an entry here names a design owner. It does not show that every integration, browser or device has been tested. Keep native state formatting, translations, permissions, capabilities and event handling. Theme tokens change presentation, not behaviour.

The [interface coverage inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json) records the source baseline and every mapping: Home Assistant Core 2026.11.1 and frontend 20261028.5. Check native behaviour again after a frontend change, and record what was actually tested using [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html). Keep native controls at 16px text with visible labels, keyboard access and readable names at 320px, and do not remove a capability explanation or recovery route to make a row compact.
